 { In this section, we'll be exploring two topics. The first is typical ways in which we can defend weak squares. The second will be on identifying opportunities to create weak squares to later be able to target them. In our starting position below, it is White to move and they have the opportunity to jump into the d6 square. In fact, this position is taken from one of my online games where neither I nor my opponent correctly responded to the weak square! Let's dive in. } 1. { -KEY- } Be3 $6 { White does not take the opportunity, and leaves Black enough time to cover the square. } ( 1. Nd6 $16 { White should have seized the moment, when after any rook move White can continue with Nxc8 and be left with a clear advantage owing to the bishop pair. } ) 
Bf8 $1 { This is the first common method of protecting your weak squares: by choosing the right piece for the job! The bishop on f8 here makes a lot of sense to deploy for this purpose, because in any case it did not have much life on g7 staring at their own locked pawn on e5. } ( 1... Bb7 $2 { In the game I unfortunately failed to react correctly, and soon landed in trouble after } 2. Nd6 $1 Re7 3. Nxb7 $16 { White gained the bishop pair, while Black's heavy pieces were also left awkwardly placed. } ) 
2. Rd2 $2 { In my experience, many players would play such a move as this one in order to try to double up rooks and finally be able to land the knight on the weak d6 square. However, here it is a bad idea as Black needs only one tempo to refute this plan. } ( 2. a4 { Even if White had played a more sensible move and tried to stabilise their knight on c4, it would not have worked: } b5 $1 3. axb5 cxb5 4. Na5 a6 $11 { Black's approach has not rid them of the weak square on d6. In fact, they have added several new weaknesses on b6, c5 and d5. However, all of the weak squares are now well-covered by Black's minor pieces. Soon Black will be able to play a move like ...Nc5 or ... Ng4, applying pressure to White's centre and minor pieces. This brings us back to the lesson from the previous section: it's not so much about weaknesses, but rather their relationship to the pieces on the board. } ) 
b5 $1 { With the d6 square unavailable, White's knight is forced to retreat to the side of the board. } 3. Na3 ( { Black can play } 3. Na3 Bxa3 { with an edge, but even stronger here is to hunt for the bishop pair with: } ) 
Ng4 $1 { It's easy to imagine White completely collapsing here. For example, the natural move to my mind is to defend the bishop with White's least active piece and play 4. Re1. However, that would be a major blunder! } 4. Re1 $2 Nxe3 5. Rxe3 Bh6 $19 { The bishop demonstrates its amazing flexibility from the f8 square, and will collect a clean exchange on the next move. The savvy positional player has developed a strong feel for the best way of configuring their pieces around the defense of any structural weaknesses of their position. In this example, the dark-squared bishop first played a defensive role before quickly switching to the attack. This example showed the simple forms of piece cover, where a player identifies the structural weakness and makes sure to control the square with one of their pieces. A more advanced version of piece cover will be shown in the next example. } 
