 { With Black to play, the position is completely equal. However, they must be alert to White's very serious positional threat. What do you think it is, and can you find a move to stop it? } 1... { -KEY- } Bf6 $1 { An important move. While the dark-squared bishop is powerless to control Black's weak square on f5, it can shut down the route along which the White knight needs to travel in order to reach it. } ( 1... Rdd7 $2 { Let's take a look at what would happen if Black failed to sense the danger. } 2. Nh4 $1 Re8 3. Rg1 $1 ( 2... Bf6 2. Nf5 $6 { White shouldn't rush, since after } Kg6 $1 { White would have nothing better than to return to h4 with check. } ) 
Bf6 4. Nf5 { White's knight has reached the weak square. That improvement in piece quality ensures that White has won the 'battle of the minor pieces', a topic that will be explored in depth in the chapter on Piece Play later in the course. Black cannot afford to continue shuffling, as White will be able to find a scheme (configuration of pieces) that will force continued progress into the Black position. There is more than one way to win, but below is the suggested plan that appeals to me: 1. Play Nf5-g3 to force ...h5-h4 in case Black does not play it themselves. Then bring the knight back to f5. 2. Play Rg1-g2, then Kh2-g1-f1-e2. 3. Play f2-f3 pawn break, and trade our f3 pawn for Black's e4 pawn. 4. Then push e3-e4 and prepare the e4-e5 pawn break, creating a protected passer with our central majority. 5. At some point, if the opportunity presents itself, pick off the weak Black pawns on the h-file. This topic of thinking in terms of schemes takes on heightened importance in situations with fewer pieces on the board, and is discussed in detail in the chapter on 'Endgame Play'. } ) 
2. Rg1 { White is not only occupying the g-file, but rather making space on e1 for their knight. } Ree8 3. Ne1 { Black is still not out of the woods, with two dangerous plans available to White. The first is to maneuver the knight to b5 from where it will put pressure on Black's d6 pawn. The second is a well-timed f3 pawn break when in the long run White may be able to mobilise their central pawn majority. } Bh4 $1 { The only move that holds the balance! Black identifies the sole weakness of White's position and targets it immediately, distracting White from executing their plan. } ( 3... Rg8 $2 { This natural exchanging move illustrates the dangers that face Black. } 4. Rxg8 $1 Rxg8 5. f3 $1 { White gets in the pawn break. Now after for example } Re8 6. fxe4 Rxe4 7. Nc2 $16 { Black faces the uncomfortable prospect of White playing e3-e4 and not only expanding on the centre, but also leaving the e3 square open for White's knight to play Nc2-e3-f5 and once again tie Black down. } ) 
4. Rg2 Rg8 $1 { Now the rook exchange is a different story, since White can no longer exchange them and play f2-f3 as in the previous variation. } 5. f3 { White might try to expand in the centre, but their piece coordination is not sufficient to trouble Black. } ( 5. Rxg8 { Here the exchange has no venom. } Rxg8 6. Rd2 { White has to defend the f2 pawn, since f2-f3 would lose to ...Bxe1! Rxe1 exf3 when the rook endgame is winning for Black! } f5 { Not the only move, but solidifying the central pawn makes sense. } 7. Nc2 { White still has the knight maneuver in mind, but now Black hatches a plan to defend the d6 pawn in a way that will maintain strong piece coordination. } Kg7 $1 { The king marches towards the centre! } 8. Na3 Kf7 9. Nb5 Ke7 $11 { All of Black's pieces are on good squares, and as a consequence they can easily manage their various structural weaknesses. Once again, we see an example of the intimate link between structure and piece play. It's also worth noting the effect of Black's active play on White's forces. Their rook is passively defending f2, while their king is trapped along the h-file. Neither side can make progress, so the position is drawn. } ) 
 ( 5. Nc2 { also has no venom, since after } Rxg2 6. Kxg2 Rg8 7. Kf1 f5 $11 { Black's rook cannot be contested along the g-file, so it can post itself on the g6 square and from there cover all possible points of attack. } ) 
Bxe1 { The most straightforward path to a draw. } 6. Rxe1 Rxg2+ 7. Kxg2 { Black must still be on guard, since the White king is threatening to activate itself via g3. } Rg8+ $1 { Black stops the idea, and now the position soon becomes lifeless. } ( 7... f5 $2 { Sloppy play would throw the game, since after } 8. fxe4 fxe4 9. Kg3 $18 { White is suddenly winning as the weak h-pawns become Black's undoing. } ) 
8. Kf2 f5 $1 { Neither side will be able to make any progress, since the only active play would be to exchange rooks along the g-file, but the resulting king and pawn endgame is drawn. For example: } 9. fxe4 fxe4 10. Rg1 Rxg1 11. Kxg1 h4 $11 { We have now taken a look at two of the most common approaches to defending weak squares: basic piece cover, and the more advanced version of cutting off the opponent's entry route. Throughout the course we will find many more instances where weak squares feature, so we will not cover more examples of this kind for now. Besides piece cover, a player can also make pawn moves in order to cover the weak squares. Let's take a look at a couple of examples of this in play. } 
