 { The worst kind of mistake when it comes to weak squares is the unforced error, either due to a gap in understanding or a lapse in focus. The following example is taken from one of my games against IM Margvelashvili. In it, I make a silly mistake that immediately swings the evaluation from completely equal to completely lost! } 1... { -KEY- } e5 $2 { In my haste to control the centre and bring my king to e7, I recklessly pushed the pawn forward by two squares. As a result, I lose any pawn cover over the d5 square. While it's true that my queenside pawn structure was already fractured, this had not been a serious problem since, in the words of Yasser Seirawan, 'a weak pawn is only weak if it can be attacked by enemy pieces!' Unfortunately, with my last move, I gifted my opponent a direct route to targeting the weak central square. } ( 1... e6 $11 { Had I maintained to keep pawn cover over the d5 square, the position would have remained equal. } ) 
2. Bc4 $1 { Ouch. With my last move, I turned the d5 square into a weak square. However, I didn't think it would be a problem as it was covered by my bishop. Unfortunately, I cannot stop White's plan of exchanging the minor pieces and consequently invading with his rooks along the d-file. } a5 { This is what I tried in the game, but other paths were no better. } ( 2... Ke7 3. Bd5 $1 Bxd5 4. Rxd5 $18 { And Black loses a pawn. } ) 
 ( { If } 2... f6 3. Bd5 Kd7 { then } 4. Be4 $18 { wins the bishop. } ) 
 ( { If } 2... Rd8 $2 { White will exchange rooks and win the pawn on f7. } 3. Rxd8 Kxd8 4. Bxf7 $18 ) 
3. Bd5 $1 Ra6 4. Bxc6+ Rxc6 5. Rd5 $1 { The weak square on d5 has been conquered, and now Black's queenside structure is in serious trouble. To make matters worse, Black's e5 pawn is under fire. } f6 { Trying to hold things together. } 6. Rhd1 $1 { Doubling up and threatening a deadly skewer with 7.Rd8+. } Ke7 7. Rd7+ Ke6 8. Rg7 $1 { White continues to slice through the Black position. The threat is to double up on the 7th rank. } c4 { Trying to get rid of my weak doubled pawns and activate my rook. } 9. Kc2 $1 { Cutting out the threat of 9...cxb3 followed by 10...Rxc3+. } Kf5 { Desperation. Black lacks a plan as both of his rooks are tied down to the defense of their pawns. My goal with this move was to protect the g6 pawn in order to be able to play ...h5 to free my rook from its defensive duty. } 10. h4 { Controlling the g5 square and preparing to push the king back to e6. } h5 11. g3 { This time White controls the f4 square, to prepare e4+. } e4 { A last attempt, hoping for a mistake from White that would allow me to push further up the board with my king. } 12. Rd5+ $1 { This nasty check not only drives my king back, but secures a pawn in the process. } Ke6 13. fxe4 $18 { I had been completely outplayed and resigned a dozen or so moves later in Margvelashvili,G - Astaneh Lopez,A (2017). This was a painful loss because of the unforced nature of my mistake, conceding a weak square against my opponent who played a perfect game once given a hook to latch on to. In your own games, I would advise that when you feel like you were positionally outplayed, try to investigate the character of your mistakes. If you are making a lot of unforced errors, this will speak to a need to develop the positional side of your game. Let's now show an example where, unlike me, the White side was alert to the positional danger and used their pawns to block the opponent's access to the weak central square. } 
