 { In the following example, White's d4 pawn is under attack. Since allowing ...cxd4 would leave White with an isolated pawn, they are thinking to either capture on c5 or advance the pawn with d4-d5. The presence of weak squares on d3 and b3 helps them to make the right decision: } 1. { -KEY- } d5 $1 { The only path to equality. The pawn advance may look aggressive, but in reality it is a mostly defensive move designed to cut off the ...Nd7-c5-d3 route for Black's knight. } ( 1. dxc5 $2 { On the surface, it's appealing for White to capture, as this would leave them with a 3 vs 2 pawn majority on the queenside. However, Black's knight now springs into activity. } Nxc5 { The b3 square is currently covered by White's knight, but that can soon change if Black plays ...b6. The real problem for White, however, is the central square on d3. If Black lands on it, White will have to immediately meet the double attack against e1 and b2. Most players will begin to panic here, and the position could quickly go downhill. For example: } 2. b4 Nd3 $1 3. Red1 Bg7 4. Ra3 { Defending the c3 pawn. } Rd8 { The position is already hopeless for White. The immediate threat of ...Nb2 has no good answer, for instance: } 5. Rb3 b6 $19 { when the knight is lost. } ) 
c4 { For their part, Black shuts out the possibility of White playing c3-c4 (consolidating their central structure) while opening up the c5 square for Black's knight in the future. However, now the c4 pawn is fragile. } 2. Re4 $1 { White immediately targets the pawn, forcing Black to shift their strategy. } Nb6 { Best, in order to defend c4 and at the same time target White's advanced central pawn. However, now Black's plan of ... Nd7-c5 is no longer possible. } 3. g3 $1 { Having defused Black's dangerous plan of targeting the weak d3 square, White now shifts focus to the kingside with the intention of undermining Black's space advantage on that flank. The game remains complex, but equal. We'll finish our analysis here, as the character of the play has moved away from our current topic of weak squares. Let's now move on to the second topic of the section: how to create weak squares. } 
