 { When seeking to create weak squares, we need to force or induce some kind of structural change. The most common method for this is shown in our first example, taken from an online game between Magnus Carlsen and GM Alexander Rustemov. This example may feel quite similar to you as the one discussed at the end of our first section, when we were speaking about the difference between weak squares and outposts. Can you find a way for White to create a weak square by force? } 1. { -KEY- } dxc5 $1 { Correct! By exchanging pawns Black will be left with a weak square on d6. } dxc5 2. Rd1 $1 { White arrives first on the d-file, positioning them well to exploit the weakness. } Qc7 3. Na3 $1 { Developing while continuing the assault on the weak square. The threat is 12.Nb5. To make matters worse for Black, there is also a chance that White will play e4-e5 at the right moment, converting the weak square into an outpost. } e5 $6 { In the game, Black likely spotted that White's plan involved not only Na3-b5, but also e4-e5. So he tried stopping this plan, opening up the scope of his light-squared bishop on c8. However, the drawback of this move is that it leaves an outpost on d5 that White may exploit in the future. } ( 3... a6 { Black could have prevented Na3-b5, but it's not without its drawbacks. After } 4. Nc4 $16 { the White knight would take aim at both d6 and the newly created weak square on b6. } ) 
4. Nb5 $1 Qb8 5. Nd6 $1 { The knight finishes its journey and arrives on the weak central square, a commonly recurring theme that we are by now quite familiar with! At a minimum, the threat is to capture on c8. The bishop pair in this instance would be very strong since there are a lot of open light-squared diagonals and White's light-squared bishop would gain tremendous strength. } Bg4 ( { There was no way to hang onto the bishop, since } 13... Be6 { would have been met by } 14. Ng5 { . } ) 
6. h3 Bxf3 { Black is forced to part ways with their bishop, a direct consequence of the loss of control over the d6 square. } 7. Qxf3 { Black now has to fight against not just the powerful knight on d6, but also against the bishop pair. } Qc7 8. Be3 b6 9. Nb5 { White retreats the knight in anticipation of 18...Rad8. } Qb7 10. h4 { Magnus seeks to chip away at the light squares around Black's king. } h5 11. Bf1 $1 { Continuing the light-squared strategy. The bishop will aim for the c4 square from where it will heavily pressure the Black kingside. } a6 12. Nd6 $18 { Black resigned. We can see from this example that things went pear-shaped for GM Rustemov the second that Magnus identified the possibility of creating a weak square on d6. That advantage led to gaining the bishop pair, and it was this dominance along the light squares that led to overwhelming pressure against Black's f7 pawn. One thing I greatly enjoyed about this game is how Magnus Carlsen's play was elegant in its simplicity. And how, while outwardly the play seemed like a level of aggression that could be easily dealt with, it proved to quickly overwhelm the defenses of his experienced Grandmaster opponent. The final two methods to explore relate to pawn advances and their impact on the structure, and in both cases we'll return to games played by Magnus Carlsen. } 
