 { Oftentimes, you may find yourself in a quiet and symmetrical position such as this one, where it appears that the game is inevitably headed towards a draw. However, sometimes hidden opportunities will exist. When Magnus Carlsen reached this position in an online game against GM Vahap Sanal he found a clever move to force Black into conceding a weak square. Can you find the strong move that White played? } 1. { -KEY- } Qb5 $1 { The only path to an advantage! Incredibly, the simple targeting of the b7 pawn makes Black's position extremely difficult to defend. } ( 1. Re1 $6 { Oftentimes positional opportunities arise only for very brief instances. This move was White's choice in the Grandmaster duel between Lupulescu,C and Parligras,M when after } c6 $11 { the possibility to create a weak square on c6 had already disappeared, and with it the position was completely equal and the players agreed to a draw in just a few more moves. } ) 
b6 { The only way to avoid immediate material losses. However, now White has achieved their objective of carving out a weak square deep in the Black position. } ( 1... Rb8 $6 2. Qa5 $16 { Black cannot avoid the loss of a pawn. } ) 
 ( 1... Qc8 $2 { is not possible due to } 2. Qxd5 $18 ) 
2. Qc6 $1 { White invades along the newly created weak square with tremendous pressure against the Black position. } Rc8 3. Re1 { White's pieces are all dominating Black's, with the exception of the knights. Black has no good way to untangle. } Kg7 ( { Pushing back the queen with } 3... Ne7 $2 { is not possible, since it disconnects Black's queen from the defense of the bishop. } 4. Qxf6 $18 ) 
4. Nf3 { Bringing the last minor piece into the game. } h6 { Preparing ...g5, in order to kick back White's bishop. } ( 4... Ne7 { is still not possible, due to a cute tactical trick at White's disposal. } 5. Bh6 $1 Kxh6 6. Qxf6 $18 { Black's king is very unsafe, and they will lose at least a pawn on the next move. } ) 
5. g4 $1 { Challenging Black's most active piece. } Nh4 6. Ne5 $6 ( { Not a bad move, though even stronger was } 6. Nxh4 Bxh4 7. Re5 { when White immediately secures the d5 pawn. } ) 
Bxe5 { Black wants to simplify as well as gain control of the weak f3 square for their knight. } ( 6... Qd6 { was best, but after } 7. Re3 { White covers his own weak square, avoiding tactical ideas like 7...Nf3+. } Qxc6 8. Nxc6 { Black has succeeded in trading queens, but in its place the knight occupies the weak square. } a5 9. Na7 Ra8 10. Nb5 { White's pressure against the Black pawn forces the creation of new weak squares. } c6 { This move loses immediately, but the position was already beyond repair. } 11. Nc7 $1 Rc8 12. Ne8 $1 { White wins a clean exchange. } ) 
7. Bxe5+ Kh7 8. Re3 $1 { Black's knight is once again robbed of mobility. In a lost position, Black collapses with his next move. } Qg5 $2 9. Qd7 $1 { Defending the g4 pawn and creating new threats. } Rf8 ( { Black resigned before Magnus had a chance to play his next move. Most probably the reason for this is that both players saw that after } 9... Rf8 9. f4 { Black has no other retreat square besides } Qd8 { but after } 11. Qxd8 Rxd8 12. Bf6 { White wins a piece.In the absence of weak squares, you will benefit from searching for ways in which you can force your opponent's hand. Once the weakness is created, you can then use this to build up an initiative.Sometimes, however, it will not be possible to directly force the creation of a weakness, and instead it will be helpful to poke and prod your opponent's position in the hopes of inducing a mistake. This theme is shown in our final example of the section. } ) 

