 { This position is taken from the online game Carlsen,M - Martinez Alcantara,J (2022). Unlike in our first two examples, here Magnus does not have a pawn exchange that creates a weak square, nor a move that forces Black to advance a pawn. In such cases, you may benefit from searching the board for a move designed to induce a pawn advance. } 1. { -KEY- } Bb5 $1 { White does not have a direct threat here, and Black is absolutely not forced to play the move he played next. However, the bishop from b5 frustrates Black's piece configuration in the centre due to the pin on the d7 knight. If Black takes no active measure here, White can build up the pressure on the queenside with b2-b4. Because of this, GM Martinez makes a commital move. } c6 { Black kicks the invading piece back, but in so doing leaves their d6 pawn undefended. This forms the basis for an eventual weak square on d6. } 2. Bf1 $1 { Black now has to worry about dxe5, which would create a weak square on d6 as well as targeting the loose pawn on b6. } d5 { Likely due to the instability of his structure, Black initiaties dynamic play with a central pawn push. } ( 2... Nf8 $2 { The structural change means that Black has lost a lot of piece flexibility. This move, for instance, would now lose material after } 3. dxe5 $1 dxe5 4. Bxb6 $18 { White wins a pawn, with the a5 pawn likely to fall next. } ) 
3. Nxe5 $1 ( 3. exd5 $6 { was the direction Magnus took in the game. It's still good for an edge, but less strong since here Black's knight takes up a strong central post on d5. } ) 
dxe4 ( 3... Nxe5 $2 { Capturing on e5 is much worse than on e4. } 4. dxe5 Qxe5 5. Bxb6 $18 { Again, the weak b6 pawn proves Black's undoing. } ) 
4. Qb3 $1 { White continues to target the vulnerable Black structure with a double attack against b6 and f7. } Rf8 5. Bc4 { Renewing the threat against the f7 pawn. } Nxe5 { Black has to get rid of the strong White knight, but now he loses control over the b6 pawn. } 6. dxe5 Rxd1 7. Rxd1 Nd5 ( 7... Qxe5 8. Qxb6 $18 { is hopeless. } ) 
8. Bxd5 cxd5 9. Qxb6 $16 { Black's queenside collapses. White's strategy was rooted in the provocative move 1.Bb5! which induced Black into creating a weakness. This type of positional play is neither flashy nor glamorous, but it is very effective. With this game, we close out our discussion on the two aspects of battling for weak squares that were covered. Firstly, we spoke about ways of using your pawns and pieces in order to control the squares directly or to cut off the route to the weak square. Secondly, we showed ways to create weak squares, through direct force or through provocation.For the final section of this chapter we'll be discussing color complexes, which are when not just one, but an entire set of colors become vulnerable. } 
 *
